{# Rendered by ops/ansible/roles/relay-health — DO NOT EDIT ON HOST.

   oauth2-proxy custom error page for the PUBLIC door. Closes the second half of
   F-334: the gate authorises an email STRING, a person can hold several, and the
   stock refusal is a bare 403 that names neither the identity nor the rule — so
   a door working exactly as configured is indistinguishable from a broken one.
   The last time that happened the escalation was "that is a big problem", which
   was the correct read on the information the page gave.

   WHAT THIS PAGE CANNOT DO, stated so nobody assumes otherwise: oauth2-proxy
   v7.6.0 renders error.html with {Title, Message, ProxyPrefix, StatusCode,
   Redirect, RequestID, Footer, Version} and NOT the email it just refused.
   Echoing the presented address — F-334's cheapest single fix — needs either an
   upstream template-data change or a shim ahead of the proxy. So this page does
   the two things it can: it names the RULE, and it prints the request id, which
   turns an unattributable refusal into one an admin can resolve in the access
   log in seconds.

   The palette is fixed rather than theme-aware on purpose: this page is served
   to someone already confused, from a host with no CSS pipeline, and a
   half-loaded theme is one more thing to doubt. #}
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
<meta charset="utf-8">
<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
<title>{{ '{{.Title}}' }}</title>
<style>
  body { margin:0; background:#12161c; color:#f2f5f8;
         font:16px/1.55 -apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,"Segoe UI",Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif; }
  main { max-width:44rem; margin:0 auto; padding:3rem 1.25rem; }
  h1 { font-size:1.5rem; margin:0 0 1rem; color:#ffffff; }
  p  { margin:0 0 1rem; }
  ul { margin:0 0 1rem; padding-left:1.2rem; }
  li { margin:0 0 .5rem; }
  .rule { background:#1c2530; border-left:4px solid #6aa9ff; padding:1rem 1.25rem; margin:0 0 1.5rem; }
  .ref { font-family:ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,monospace; font-size:.9rem;
         color:#cfd8e3; background:#1c2530; padding:.75rem 1rem; border-radius:4px;
         word-break:break-all; }
  a { color:#8fc0ff; }
  .muted { color:#b6c2d0; font-size:.9rem; }
</style>
</head>
<body>
<main>
{{ '{{ if eq .StatusCode 403 }}' }}
  <h1>You signed in, but this address is not on the access list</h1>

  <div class="rule">
    Access to these dashboards is granted <strong>one email address at a time</strong>.
    Your sign-in worked — the address it carried is simply not one of the
    addresses on the list for this site.
  </div>

  <p>The usual cause is having more than one address. If you were invited at a
  work address and signed in with a personal account (or the reverse), the door
  sees a different person.</p>

  <ul>
    <li><a href="{{ '{{.ProxyPrefix}}' }}/sign_out">Sign out</a>, then sign in again
        choosing the account whose address you were invited at.</li>
    <li>If you only have one address, or you are not sure which one was used,
        reply to the email that invited you and quote the reference below.
        Adding a second address to your access is something an administrator
        can do.</li>
  </ul>

  <p class="muted">Reference for whoever helps you — it identifies this exact
  request in the access log, including the address that was presented:</p>
  <p class="ref">{{ '{{.RequestID}}' }}</p>
{{ '{{ else }}' }}
  <h1>{{ '{{.Title}}' }}</h1>
  <p>{{ '{{.Message}}' }}</p>
  <p><a href="{{ '{{.Redirect}}' }}">Try again</a></p>
  <p class="muted">Reference: <span class="ref">{{ '{{.RequestID}}' }}</span></p>
{{ '{{ end }}' }}
</main>
</body>
</html>
